Metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) have r1ece4 ved great guing utilization in diverse applications. 511 The large diversity in structure and pore size, high surface area, adsorption affinity, and selective penetration have made MOFs attractive as highly efficient adsorbents1218 and advanced separation media.1927
Owing to well-defined pore structures and small pore apertures, MOFs should have great potential to selectively adsorb and separate specially sized or shaped targets on the basis of the molecular sieve effect or size exclusion mechanism.
Solid-phase microextraction (SPME) is a solvent-free sampling technique first introduced by Pawliszyn and co-workers in 1990.28 Although a number of SPME coatings are commercially available, their performance is not always satisfactory for the extraction of complex real samples because of their instability in some organic solvents and more importantly the limited selectivity of the coatings. Cui et al.29 have made the first MOF-coated fiber for SPME by the in situ hydrothermal growth method for selective extraction of the benzene homologues from indoor air samples with large enhancement factors.
Porous-layer open-tubular (PLOT) columns are usually used in gas chromatography (GC) for the separation of fixed gases, light hydrocarbons, and volatile solvents.30 However, only a small number of materials such as nanoparticles of alumina, carbons, silica, molecular sieves, and a few porous polymers are currently suitable as the coatings for PLOT columns, limiting the wide applications of PLOTs.30 Recently, utilizations of MOFs as membranes for sieving gas molecules such as $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{31-38}$ and as the coatings for PLOT columns in GC for sieving and separating small organic molecules (i.e., alkanes)39,40 have been reported. Owing to the pore aperture selectivity of MOFs, combination of various MOFs as tandem molecular sieves for extraction in conjunction with chromatographic separation would be promising for the analysis of complicated samples; however, to the best of our knowledge, it has not been explored so far.
Here we report the design of MOF-based tandem molecular sieves as a dual platform for SPME in combination with highresolution GC (HR-GC) to achieve both selective extraction and HR-GC separation of target analytes in complex matrixes. For this purpose, we chose $n$ -alkanes as the target analytes because alkanes are important constituents of raw chemicals in the petroleum and chemical industry. The separation of $n$ -alkanes from branched isomers or benzene homologues is a very important process in petroleum refining to improve the gasoline octane number and low-temperature properties of aviation fuels.4146 Moreover, fuel and fuel emissions exist as a widespread source of nonoccupational exposure to the general population. This $n$ -alkane signature is useful in distinguishing typical fuel biomarkers (e.g., benzene and toluene) from other confounding exposure sources such as cigarette smoke. For these reasons, attempts for accurate quantification of volatile $n$ -alkanes have been made to access the exposure to the environmental pollution.47,48
Petroleum-based fuels are complex as they contain hundreds and thousands of components, such as linear alkanes and alkenes, branched alkanes and alkenes, cyclanes, cyclenes, aromatic compounds, and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons. Sample pretreatment and GC separation of such complicated samples is a great challenge. The main purpose of this work is to develop an effective MOF-based tandem molecular sieve platform for highly selective analysis of volatile $n$ -alkanes in petroleum-based fuel and biological fluid using an MOF-coated headspace SPME fiber in combination with MOF-coated capillary GC.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemicals and Reagents. All chemicals were at least of analytical grade and used without further treatment. Hydrochloric acid and nitric acid were purchased from the Tianjin Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute (Tianjin, China). $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ 3 $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ 2-methylimidazole, benzimidazole, terephthalic acid, and benzenetricarboxylic acid were from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China). $\mathrm{Zn}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\big)_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ methanol, ethanol, dichloromethane $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\right)$ , and $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) were from Tianjin Standard Science and Technology Co. (Tianjin, China). Hexane $(\geq98\%)$ , heptane $(\geq98\%)$ , octane $(\geq96\%)$ , nonane $(\geq99\%)$ , tetradecane $(\geq98\%)$ , and $p$ -xylene were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Ethylbenzene $(\geq99\%)$ was purchased from the Tianjin Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute (Tianjin, China). $m$ -Xylene $(\geq95\%)$ was purchased from the Beijing Chemical Reagent No. 1 Plant (Beijing, China). $o$ -Xylene was purchased from the Tianjin Chemical Reagent No. 2 Plant (Tianjin, China). Ultrapure water $\left(18.2\mathrm{M}\bar{\Omega}\mathrm{cm}\right)$ ) was obtained from a WaterPro water purification system (Labconco Corp., Kansas City, MO). Stock solutions of the analytes $(500\mathrm{mg}\hat{\mathrm{L}}^{-1}\dot{\phantom{0}})$ ) were prepared using tetradecane as the solvent. All the stock solutions were prepared by step-by-step dilution with tetradecane just before use.
Instrumentation. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) micrographs were recorded on a Shimadzu SS-550 scanning electron microscope at $15.0\ \mathrm{kV}.$ . The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded with a $\mathrm{D/max}{-2500}$ diffractometer (Rigaku, Japan) using $\mathrm{Cu\K@}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) experiments were performed on a PTC-10A thermal gravimetric analyzer (Rigaku, Japan) from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a ramp rate of $\mathrm{\check{1}0^{\circ}C\dot{m i n}^{-1}}$
GC measurements were performed on an Agilent 7890 GC system with a flame ionization detector (FID). Nitrogen $(99.999\%)$ ) was used as the carrier gas. Measurements for GC/ mass spectrometry (GC/MS) were performed on an Agilent 7890 GC-Agilent 5975c inert MSD with a triple-axis detector. Helium $(99.999\%)$ ) was used as the carrier gas for the GC/MS system.
A commercial SPME manual holder and the fiber coated with $65\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ of PDMS/DVB (Supelco, Bellefonte, PA) was used for comparison of SPME. The fiber was conditioned in the GC injection port according to the manufacturer. A commercial GC capillary column $\left(\mathrm{HP-5,~}30\mathrm{~m~length}\times0.25\mathrm{~mm~i.d.}\times0.25\mu\mathrm{m}\right)$ (Agilent Technology) was used for comparison of GC separation.
Synthesis, Activation, and Characterization of MOF Crystals. MOF-199 nanocrystals were synthesized at room temperature according to Tranchemontagne et al.49 Briefly, benzenetricarboxylic acid ( $\cdot125\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.595\ \mathrm{mmol},$ ) was mixed in $12~\mathrm{mL}$ of a 1:1:1 volume ratio of DMF/ $\mathrm{'EtOH/H_{2}O}$ $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(215\mathrm{mg},1.08\mathrm{mmol})$ ) was mixed with $12\mathrm{mL}$ of the same solvent, and the mixtures were combined with stirring. Triethylamine $(125\mu\mathrm{L})$ was added to the reaction mixture, which was stirred at $600~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $3\ \mathrm{h}.$ . The product was collected by filtration and washed with DMF three times. The MOF-199 nanocrystal was dispersed in $10\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF for SPME coating. Samples for XRD and TGA were dried at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the air.
ZIF-8 nanocrystals were synthesized at room temperature according to Cravillon et al.50 Briefly, a solution of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ 3 $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $293.3\mathrm{mg},0.987\mathrm{mmol},$ ) in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol was rapidly poured into a solution of 2-methylimidazole $(648.9\mathrm{~mg},7.904$ mmol) in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol under stirring with a magnetic bar at $600~\mathrm{rpm}$ . The mixture slowly turned turbid, and after $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ the nanocrystals were obtained. The nanocrystals were separated from the milky dispersion by centrifugation at $10000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ and then washed with fresh methanol. The procedure was repeated three times to remove the unreacted 2-methylimidazole from ZIF-8. ZIF-8 nanocrystals were dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol for preparing the ZIF-8-coated SPME fiber and ZIF-8- coated capillary column. Samples for XRD and TGA were dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the air.
ZIF-7 nanocrystals were synthesized at room temperature according to Li et al.51 Briefly, a solution of $\mathrm{Zn}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{2}^{-}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(302\mathrm{mg},1.02\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ in $50\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF was rapidly poured into a solution of benzimidazole $(769\mathrm{~mg},6.51\mathrm{~mmol})$ in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF under stirring with a magnetic bar at $600~\mathrm{rpm}$ . After the reaction mixture sat at room temperature for $^{48\mathrm{~h,~}}$ the product was separated by centrifugation at $10000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ and then washed with fresh methanol. The procedure was repeated three times to remove the unreacted benzimidazole from ZIF-7. The resulting ZIF-7 nanocrystals were dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol for preparing the ZIF-7-coated SPME fiber and ZIF-7- coated capillary column. Samples for XRD and TGA were dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the air.
MOF-5 nanocrystals were synthesized at room temperature according to Tranchemontagne et al.49 Briefly, terephthalic acid $\left(101.3\mathrm{~mg},\ 0.61\mathrm{~mmol}\right)$ was dissolved in $32~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(OAc)}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(339.8\mathrm{\mg},\ 1.55\mathrm{\mmol}\right),$ was dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The zinc salt solution was added to the organic solution with stirring at $600~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $\{\dot{s}\ \mathrm{min}\$ . The product was collected by filtration and then washed with fresh $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ . The procedure was repeated three times. MOF-5 nanocrystals were dispersed in $10\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ for GC capillary coating. Samples for XRD and TGA were dried at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the air.
MIL-101 was synthesized according to Ferey et al.52 Typically, $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\dot{\mathrm{O}}$ $\left(800\ \mathrm{mg},2.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\right),$ ), terephthalic acid (332 $\mathrm{mg},2.0\ \mathrm{mmol},$ ), and HF $(0.4~\mathrm{mL},2.0~\mathrm{mmol})$ were mixed with ultrapure water $(9.5~\mathrm{mL})$ ). The obtained mixture was transferred to a Teflon-lined bomb. Then the Teflon-lined bomb was sealed, placed in an oven, and left at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . A green crystalline solid was thus obtained. After thorough washing with DMF, the solid was collected by centrifugation at $8000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $\ensuremath{5}\mathrm{{min}}$ . The procedure was repeated three times to remove the unreacted terephthalic acid from MIL-101. The solid was dispersed in
$10\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol for GC capillary coating. Samples for XRD and TGA were dried in the air at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Fabrication of the MOF-Coated SPME Fibers. Stainless steel wires (Shanghai Gaoge Industrial and Trade Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China) with a length of $20\mathrm{cm}$ used to fabricate the SPME fibers were pretreated according to Cui et al.29 Briefly, one end of the stainless steel wire $3.0\mathrm{cm}$ in length) was immersed in aqua regia $\left(\mathrm{HCl}{:}\mathrm{HNO}_{3}=3{:}1,\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\right)$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . The surface of the wire gradually turned rough while small gas bubbles were released during etching. The etched part of the stainless steel wire was taken out of the aqua regia and washed gently by ultrapure water $\left(18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega\mathrm{cm}\right)$ . Then the etched stainless steel wire was assembled into a $5\mu\mathrm{L}$ microsyringe and conditioned at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ under nitrogen in the GC injector before coating.
The MOF-coated SPME fibers were fabricated by a layer-bylayer deposition method. Briefly, the MOF-199, ZIF-8, and ZIF-7 nanocrystals were washed with DMF (or methanol) three times and dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF (or methanol) solution. The conditioned stainless steel fiber was dipped into the MOF nanocrystal solution, with stirring for $20\ \mathrm{s},$ removed from the coating solution, and allowed to dry under nitrogen at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ in the GC injector. The process was repeated 10 times. Prior to performance of the extractions, the coated fibers were conditioned at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the GC injection port for $^\mathrm{1h}$ to remove residual solvents from the fiber support.
Fabrication of the MOF-Coated Capillary Column for GC Separation. The fused silica capillary (Yongnian Optic Fiber Plant, Hebei, China) was treated according to the following recipe before dynamic coating: the capillary was washed with $1\mathbf{M}$ NaOH for $^{\mathrm{2h,}}$ ultrapure water for $30\mathrm{min}$ , 0.1 M HCl for $^{2\mathrm{h},}$ and ultrapure water until the outflow reached $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ . The capillary was dried with nitrogen purge at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
MOF-5, ZIF-8, ZIF-7, and MIL-101 were coated onto the pretreated capillary column by a dynamic coating method and characterized by scanning electron microscopy. Briefly, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol suspension of ZIF-8 (or suspension of ZIF-7, MOF-5, and MIL-101) was first filled into the capillary column under gas pressure and then pushed through the column at a velocity of $\mathrm{{\dot{4}0c m m i n^{-1}}}$ to leave a wet coating layer on the inner wall of the capillary column. To avoid acceleration of the solution plug near the end of the column, a $\mathrm{1m}$ long buffer tube was attached to the capillary column end as a restrictor. After coating, the capillary column settled for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ for conditioning under nitrogen. Further conditioning of the capillary column was carried out using a temperature program: $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ , ramp from 30 to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a rate of $3\mathrm{\hat{~}C\dot{m i n}^{-1}}$ , and $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The temperature program was repeated three times.
Collection of Samples. A petroleum-based fuel sample was obtained from a local gas station. A human serum sample was provided by a local hospital. The samples were collected in precleaned glass bottles, which were thoroughly washed with detergents, water, methanol, and ultrapure water and dried before use, and the samples were kept in a refrigerator at $-4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before use.
Determination of Enhancement Factors. The enhancement factor (EF) was defined as the ratio of the sensitivity of an analyte after extraction to that before extraction (i.e., by direct injection of $1\mu\mathrm{L}$ of standard solution) using the chromatographic peak area for quantification.
Analytical Procedures. A $100\mu\mathrm{L}$ aliquot of standard solution (or human serum) was introduced to a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ gastight sealed glass vial and homogenized ultrasonically at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\ensuremath{5}\mathrm{{min}}$ . The petroleum-based fuel sample was diluted 500-fold using tetradecane as the solvent before extraction. A $1.0\mu\mathrm{L}$ aliquot of the diluted fuel sample was introduced to a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ gastight sealed glass vial and homogenized in an ultrasonic bath at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 min. Headspace SPME was performed by piercing the needle of the SPME device through the septum and pushing the SPME fiber to expose it in the headspace of the vial. After extraction for $20\ \mathrm{min}$ (Figure S1, Supporting Information), the needle was removed from the vial and immediately transferred to the GC injection port for thermal desorption at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\ensuremath{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~min~}}$ and subsequent analysis. Between two extractions, the fiber was conditioned at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\ensuremath{5}\mathrm{{min}}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of the MOF-Coated SPME Fibers and MOF-Coated GC Capillary. To demonstrate the proof-ofconcept, we chose five MOFs with various pore and poreaperture sizes to investigate the pore-aperture selectivity and molecular sieve effect or size exclusion effect. For this purpose, MOF-199, ZIF-8, and ZIF-7 with a pore aperture size of 9.0, 3.4, and $2.9\mathrm{~\AA},$ respectively, were utilized as the fiber coatings for SPME, while MOF-5, MIL-101, ZIF-8, and ZIF-7 with a pore aperture of 11, 12, 3.4, and $2.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ respectively, were employed as the stationary phases for GC separation.
The prepared MOF-199, ZIF-8, ZIF-7, MOF-5, and MIL-101 crystals were pretreated at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for XRD characterization. The successful preparation of the above MOFs was confirmed by comparing the experimental XRD patterns with those simulated (Figures S2 S6, Supporting Information). TGA shows that the MOF-199, ZIF-8, ZIF-7, MOF-5, and MIL-101 were stable up to $280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S7, Supporting Information), $380^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S8, Supporting Information), $480^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information), $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S10, Supporting Information), and $320~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information), respectively.
The MOF coatings on the fabricated SPME fibers were not only smooth and homogeneous (Figure 1), but also firm and mechanically stable (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The MOF-coated GC capillary columns exhibited smooth and homogeneous MOF coatings on the inner wall of the fused silica capillary (Figures S13 S16, Supporting Information).
Selectivity of MOFs in SPME. To show the selectivity of the chosen small-pore MOFs, we applied the MOF-coated SPME fibers for extraction of hydrocarbons in a petroleum-based fuel sample. A commercial SPME fiber with PDMS/DVB coating was also used for comparison. The PDMS/DVB coated fiber gave little selectivity of benzene homologues, linear alkanes, and branched alkanes, so the chromatograms were too complicated to identify (Figure $^{2\mathrm{a},\mathrm{b}}.$ ; Figure S17, Supporting Information). The MOF-199-coated SPME fiber did not show much selectivity either because of its large square-shaped pores of $9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times9\mathring{\mathrm{A}},^{5}$ 3 which extracted not only linear alkanes, but also branched alkanes and benzene homologues. As a result, light alkanes were extracted more than heavy alkanes because of their higher volatility (Figure 2c,d; Figure S18, Supporting Information). Nevertheless, the ZIF-8- and ZIF-7-coated SPME fibers gave good selectivity for hydrocarbons (Figure $^{\mathrm{2e,f,g,h;}}$ Figures S19 and S20, Supporting Information). The ZIF-8-coated SPME fiber exhibited great selectivity for $n$ -alkanes over branched alkanes because of its suitability of pore aperture $(3.4\mathrm{\AA})^{54}$ for $n$ -alkanes (Figure 2e), while the ZIF-7-coated SPME fiber could not extract alkanes whether they were linear or branched as a result of its too small pore aperture $(2.9\mathrm{~\AA~})^{4}$ (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ ). Although the sizes of the benzene homologues are larger than the pore apertures of ZIF-8 and ZIF-7, benzene homologues were also effectively extracted by ZIF-8- and ZIF-7-coated fibers because of the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking between benzene homologues and the imidazole group of the ZIF-8 and ZIF-7 surface (Figure $^{\mathrm{2f,h}},$ ). Considering differences in the molecular sizes of benzene homologues and alkane homologues, and the pore-aperture sizes of the MOFs, the size selectivity of the MOF-199-, ZIF-8-, and ZIF-7-coated SPME fibers is obvious for the SPME of hydrocarbons.
Selectivity of MOFs in GC Separation. To reveal the molecular sieving effect of MOFs in chromatographic separation, capillaries coated with different pore-aperture MOFs (MOF-5, ZIF-8, and ZIF-7) were fabricated for GC separation. Owing to the difference in pore aperture, capillaries coated with MOF-5, ZIF-8, and ZIF-7 crystals exhibited different abilities to separate benzene homologues and alkane homologues (Figure 3). The typical elution sequence of hydrocarbons on a traditional stationary phase such as ( $5\%$ phenyl)methylpolysiloxane usually follows the order of the boiling points (Figure 3a). A similar elution sequence was observed on the MOF-5-coated capillary with large pore channels without the molecular sieving effect (or size exclusion effect) (Figure 3b). Meanwhile, the ZIF-7-coated capillary did not give baseline separation of light hydrocarbons because of its too small pore aperture inaccessible to these hydrocarbons. Benzene homologues had a little stronger retention than alkanes with the same number of carbons because of $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking (Figure 3c). The ZIF-8-coated capillary not only showed strong power to sieve linear alkanes from branched alkanes owing to the narrow pore windows, but also offered excellent features for high-resolution GC separation of linear alkanes because of van der Waals interaction between linear alkanes and the hydrophobic inner surfaces of the micropores40 (Figure 3d). Benzene homologues had weaker retention than the linear alkanes with identical carbon numbers on the ZIF-8-coated capillary column (which was different from the ZIF-7 capillary column) because of the inaccessible pore of ZIF-8 to benzene homologues. Meanwhile, methyl-substituted or $n$ -alkyl-substituted benzene homologues gave broad peaks on the ZIF-8 capillary column (Figure 3e).
Synergy of MOF-Based Tandem Molecular Sieves in the SPME/GC Separation System. Although an obvious molecular sieving effect was observed both in SPME and in GC separation, effective separation of target analytes in complicated matrixes was still difficult by individual MOF-based SPME or GC. Analytes with small differences in the interaction with the MOF coating are impossible to distinguish in the SPME procedure alone. It is also difficult to analyze complicated samples by GC alone without sample pretreatments owing to the limited column capacity and selectivity. Nevertheless, an appropriate combination of an MOF-coated fiber for SPME with an MOFcoated capillary column for GC separation provides an effective MOF-based tandem molecular sieve platform for selective microextraction and high-resolution GC separation of target analytes for real complex sample analysis.
To address this point, a petroleum-based fuel was analyzed for $n$ -alkanes using the tandem molecular sieve platform by combining a ZIF-8-coated fiber for SPME with a ZIF-8-coated capillary column for high-resolution GC separation. The use of a commercial PDMS/DVB fiber for SPME with subsequent GC separation on a commercial HP-5 capillary column made selective determination of $\overset{\cdot}{n}$ -alkanes in such complex petroleum-based fuel difficult because of interference from a lot of coextracted substances (Figure 2; Figure S17, Supporting Information). The same is true for the use of an MOF-199-coated fiber instead of a PDMS/DVB fiber for SPME (Figure 2; Figure S18, Supporting Information). However, an elegant combination of a ZIF-8- coated fiber for SPME with a ZIF-8-coated capillary column for subsequent GC separation resulted in simple and clean gas chromatograms of high quality (Figure 4; Figure S21, Supporting Information), showing an effective extraction and high-resolution separation of the $n$ -alkanes in the petroleum-based fuel sample with high selectivity and sensitivity. Such high selectivity for the separation and determination of linear alkanes in a complex petroleum-based fuel sample originated from the ability of the ZIF-8-coated SPME fiber to sieve $n$ -alkanes from branched alkanes (Figures 2e; Figure S19, Supporting Information) and the ZIF-8-coated capillary to distinguish $n$ -alkanes from benzene homologues in GC (Figure 3e).
The salient features of the proposed MOF-based tandem molecular sieve platform were further revealed by extracting and separating volatile $n$ -alkanes in a human serum sample, a significant analytical task to assess nonoccupational petroleumbased fuel exposure. The tandem molecular sieve platform by combining a ZIF-8-coated fiber for SPME with a ZIF-8-coated capillary column for subsequent GC separation also gave highquality chromatograms to analyze human serum for $n$ -alkanes because of high tandem selectivity (Figure 5).
Analytical Performance of the Developed MOF-Based Tandem Molecular Sieves for SPME/GC. The analytical figures of merit of the developed tandem ZIF-8 molecular sieves for SPME and ${\bf G}{\bf C}/{\bf M}{\bf S}$ determination of volatile $n$ -alkanes are summarized in Table 1. The linearity of the overall method ranged from 10 to $62500~\mathrm{ng~L}^{-1}$ with a correlation coefficients $\left(r^{2}\right)$ of $0.9903-0.9997.$ The limits of detection (LODs; $S/\mathrm{N}=3\AA$ ) varied from $0.46~\mathrm{{ng}~L^{-1}}$ for nonane to $1.06~\mathrm{{ng}~L^{-1}}$ for hexane. The limits of quantification (LOQs; $S/\mathrm{N}=10$ ) varied from 1.53 $\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ for nonane to $3.54~\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ for hexane. The ZIF-8-coated SPME fiber also offered large EFs from 235 (hexane) to 1212 (nonane). The relative standard deviations of the retention time, peak area, peak height, and half peak width for five repli cate determinations of the tested $n$ -alkanes at $30~\mathrm{\ng~L^{-1}}$ were $0.02\mathrm{-}0.26\%,1.9\mathrm{-}8.6\%,1.4\mathrm{-}6.0\%,$ and $1.3\mathrm{-}7.2\%$ , respectively (Table 2).
Application to Real Samples. In recent years, with the development of the vehicle industry, the general population was exposed increasingly to petroleum-based fuels. The developed tandem ZIF-8 molecular sieves as the SPME fiber and GC capillary were applied to the analysis of petroleum-based fuel and human serum for the determination of volatile $n$ -alkanes, including hexane, heptane, octane, and nonane. The analytical results are given in Table 3 and Figure 6. The recoveries of hexane, heptane, octane, and nonane for the developed tandem ZIF-8 molecular sieves as the SPME fiber and GC capillary ranged from $82\%$ to $105\%$ . Figure 6 shows chromatograms of the $n$ -alkane standard gas mixture and the three samples with the tandem ZIF-8 molecular sieves for SPME and GC separation.
## ’CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we have reported the design of MOF-based tandem molecular sieves as a dual platform for selective microextraction and high-performance chromatographic separation of target analytes in complex matrixes. The elegant combination of a ZIF-8-coated fiber for SPME with a ZIF-8-coated capillary column for subsequent GC separation has demonstrated such a dual platform for selective and efficient determination of volatile $n$ -alkanes in complex petroleum-based fuel and human serum samples. Besides, a combination of a ZIF-7-coated SPME fiber with an MIL-101-coated capillary column had strong power to selectively extract benzene homologues and high-resolution separation of substituted benzene derivatives (Figure S22, Table S1, Supporting Information) because of the good selectivity of the ZIF-7 SPME fiber toward benzene homologues against linear and branched alkanes (Figure $^{2\mathrm{g},\mathrm{h}}.$ ) and the excellent performance of the MIL-101-coated capillary for the separation of benzene homologues and isomers of substituted benzene derivatives (Figure S23, Supporting Information).23 The large diversity in structure and pore size allows various combinations of MOFs for designing an MOF-based tandem molecular sieve platform to achieve different selectivities in extraction and chromatographic separation and to solve headache problems in complex real sample analysis.
